  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "When an owner seeks a soft-brand or collection brand for a hotel conversion in the Caribbean and Latin America (CALA), the goal is typically to tap into a global reservation engine, distribution system, and loyalty program while retaining the property’s unique identity, design, and local character. \n\nSoft brands have experienced rapid growth across the CALA region because many properties—ranging from historic urban boutique hotels to oceanfront all-inclusive resorts—do not fit neatly into rigid \"hard-brand\" standard templates.\n\n---\n\n### **1. Marriott International**\nMarriott dominates conversion activity in the CALA region through several distinct collection brands tailored to different tiers:\n\n* **The Luxury Collection:** High-end luxury resorts and iconic heritage properties. Popular for historic haciendas, iconic Caribbean luxury resorts, and upscale all-inclusives.\n* **Autograph Collection:** Upper-upscale and luxury independent hotels. This is one of the most widely recognized collection brands in CALA, frequenting resort markets in Mexico, Costa Rica, and the Caribbean.\n* **Tribute Portfolio:** Upscale boutique and lifestyle properties. Ideal for hip urban conversions, boutique beach properties, and indie lifestyle hotels looking for a distinct visual identity.\n\n### **2. Hilton**\nHilton offers a strong three-tiered soft brand portfolio backed by the *Hilton Honors* ecosystem:\n\n* **LXR Hotels & Resorts:** Ultra-luxury collection for distinct, world-class resort properties and top-tier destination hotels.\n* **Curio Collection by Hilton:** Upper-upscale independent properties. Very active in Mexico, Central America, and the Caribbean for hoteliers looking for curated, local luxury experiences.\n* **Tapestry Collection by Hilton:** Upscale soft brand for unique, vibrant hotels, often used in secondary/tertiary urban hubs or boutique leisure destinations.\n\n### **3. Hyatt Hotels Corporation**\nHyatt's collection portfolio focuses heavily on lifestyle and experiential hospitality:\n\n* **The Unbound Collection by Hyatt:** Upper-upscale to luxury properties with a rich story, history, or unique architectural concept.\n* **Destination by Hyatt:** Focuses on upscale and luxury independent resorts and residences deeply rooted in local destination experiences.\n* **JdV by Hyatt (Joie de Vivre):** Vibrant, neighborhood-centric boutique lifestyle hotels.\n\n### **4. IHG Hotels & Resorts**\nIHG has significantly expanded its conversion offerings in LatAm and the Caribbean:\n\n* **Vignette Collection:** IHG’s luxury and lifestyle soft brand. It targets high-end boutique properties in key Latin American cities and resort destinations looking to join *IHG One Rewards*.\n* **Iberostar Partnership:** In resort and all-inclusive segments, IHG’s commercial alliance with Iberostar serves as a strategic conversion channel across the Caribbean.\n\n### **5. Accor**\nAccor brings deep international reach and strong lifestyle credentials, especially across South America and Mexico:\n\n* **Emblems Collection:** Ultra-luxury boutique collection brand.\n* **MGallery Hotel Collection:** Upper-upscale/luxury boutique hotels with a strong emphasis on storytelling and heritage.\n* **Handwritten Collection:** Midscale-to-upscale collection for charming independent properties.\n\n### **6. Wyndham Hotels & Resorts**\n* **Registry Collection Hotels:** Luxury soft brand aimed at high-end independent resorts, all-inclusives, and boutique retreats.\n* **Trademark Collection by Wyndham:** Upscale and upper-midscale soft brand, popular for budget-conscious conversions in urban or secondary regional markets.\n\n### **7. Choice Hotels**\n* **Ascend Hotel Collection:** One of the original soft brands, well-suited for midscale to upscale historic, boutique, or resort properties across Mexico and the Caribbean.\n\n---\n\n### **8. Independent Representation Alliances**\nFor owners who prefer **not** to franchise with a major chain or adopt royalty fee structures, independent global representation networks are common alternatives:\n\n* **Leading Hotels of the World (LHW):** Uncompromising luxury standard; offers global marketing and sales without chain branding.\n* **Preferred Hotels & Resorts:** Divided into collections (Legend, L.V.X., Lifestyle, Connect) depending on property tier.\n* **Small Luxury Hotels of the World (SLH):** Focuses on intimate luxury boutique properties. *(Note: SLH properties now integrate with Hilton’s distribution and loyalty channels).*\n\n---\n\n### **Key Decision Factors for Owners in CALA**\n\n1. **All-Inclusive Capabilities:** In the Caribbean and Mexico, all-inclusive conversions are a major driver. Collections like *Autograph*, *Luxury Collection*, and *Registry Collection* have built dedicated support frameworks for all-inclusive models.\n2. **Loyalty Program Engine:** *Marriott Bonvoy*, *Hilton Honors*, and *World of Hyatt* carry immense power in driving high-value U.S. and North American leisure guests to CALA destinations.\n3. **PIP (Property Improvement Plan) Requirements:** Soft brands generally offer more flexible design guidelines compared to hard flags, but PIP costs vary significantly based on whether the brand is classified as Luxury vs. Upscale.\n4. **Fees vs. Performance:** Soft brands generally charge franchise fees comparable to hard brands, so owners often weigh royalty costs against the projected uplift in Direct ADR (Average Daily Rate) and occupancy.",
